@meta
id: 14-05
title: Lines eight to nineteen
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-range
par: 28
minutes: 5
vscode: select the twelve lines by hand, Ctrl-H, "in selection"
vimrc: set number
open: seed.sql

@brief
Finance ran a euro pilot for March. The twelve March rows in this seed
file were raised in EUR, and whoever wrote the seed put `'GBP'` on all
of them. February and April really were GBP and are correct.

A pattern cannot tell a March `'GBP'` from a February one; the text is
identical. What separates them is where they are: lines 8 to 19. A
range can say that, and this lesson runs with line numbers on so you
can see it.

Change the currency on those twelve lines and no others.

@keys
| `:8,19s/old/new/` `Enter` | substitute on lines 8 through 19 inclusive |
| `:8s/old/new/` `Enter` | a single line by number |
| `%` | the range you already know — it is `1,$` |

@hints
The range goes where the `%` went in the last lesson: between the colon
and the `s`. Two numbers with a comma between them.
--
Both ends are inclusive. Line 8 is the first March row and line 19 is
the last, so the range is `8,19`.
--
`:8,19s/'GBP'/'EUR'/` and Enter. No `g` needed; each row has one
currency.

@solution
    :8,19s/'GBP'/'EUR'/<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-four keystrokes and you never moved the cursor.

`8,19` is the plainest range there is: two line numbers, both
inclusive. It is the right tool when the thing that distinguishes the
lines you want is their position rather than their content. That is
more common than it sounds. Blocks of test data, one section of a
changelog, the rows of one month in an export.

The trap with numeric ranges is that the numbers are only true until
the file changes. Insert a line above the block and `8,19` now covers
the wrong rows. Use them for a single command you run now, in a file
you are looking at. The next two lessons give you ranges that are
anchored to the cursor and to marks, which survive that kind of drift.

@start seed.sql
-- Refund seed data for the reconciliation tests.
-- Amounts are in minor units. March 2026 was the EUR pilot.

INSERT INTO refunds (id, order_id, amount, currency, raised_at) VALUES
